eans every vertex has a neighbor. The independence number `indepNum G` is the maximum size of an independent set, and the edge cover number `edgeCoverNumber G` is the minimum number of edges to cover all vertices.\n\n\n**Verifying the core inequality**\n\nConsidering Gallai's identities relating independence number, vertex cover, edge cover, and matching number. Utilizing $\\nu(G) \\le \\tau(G)$, deducing that the edge cover number $\\rho(G) \\ge$ the independence number $\\alpha(G)$. Attempting to confirm this general inequality with specific graph instances.\n\nVerified the inequality holds for $K_3$ (triangle graph), where $\\alpha(K_3) = 1$ and $\\rho(K_3) = 2$. Next considering a star graph $K_{1,k}$. Determined that, in $K_{1,k}$, independence number $\\alpha(K_{1,k}) = k$. Determining the edge cover number of the star graph, focusing on covering all leaves.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c '\nimport networkx as nx\nfor n in range(2, 8):\n    for G in nx.graph_atlas_g():\n        if G.number_of_nodes() == n:\n            if min(dict(G.degree()).values()) > 0:\n                # no isolated vertices\n                alpha = nx.algorithms.clique.max_weight_clique(nx.complement(G), weight=None)[1]\n                # min edge cover\n                # min_edge_cover in nx\n                mec = nx.min_edge_cover(G)\n                rho = len(mec)\n                if alpha > rho:\n                    print(f\"Counterexample! n={n}, alpha={alpha}, rho={rho}, edges={list(G.edges())}\")\nprint(\"Done checking Atlas\")\n'","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_1facfd37","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Testing with Python networkx","toolSummary":"Test alpha <= rho on Graph Atlas"}}]}
