{"step_index":49,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:45:36Z","thinking":"**Verifying a Graph Theorem**\n\nConsidering a graph $G$ with an independent set $S$ and an edge cover $C$, the goal is to prove $|S| \\le |C|$. This seems potentially difficult to prove directly for arbitrary $S$ and $C$.\n\n\n**Establishing Set Cardinality Relationship**\n\nEach edge in the cover can contain at most one vertex from the independent set; otherwise, they are adjacent, contradicting independence. This allows construction of an injective map from the independent set to the edge cover, since each vertex in the independent set must be covered by an edge in the cover. Therefore, the size of the independent set cannot exceed the size of the edge cover.\n\n\n**Confirming Mathematical Derivation**\n\nThe reasoning establishes $|S| \\le |C|$ for any independent set $S$ and edge cover $C$ by showing an injective mapping. This holds because the independence of $S$ prevents any edge in $C$ from covering multiple vertices from $S$. Then, by taking the maximum independent set $S$, it follows that the independence number is bounded by the size of any edge cover. The minimal edge cover then becomes the edge cover number, leading to the final inequality.\n\n**Addressing Potential Edge Cases**\n\nChecking hypotheses, the assumption includes graphs with edge covers. Therefore, cases where edge covers do not exist are excluded. Also checking graph definitions for `indepNum` and `edgeCoverNumber` to be sure the definitions align with standard graph theory.\n\n\n**Analyzing Edge Cover Number**\n\nThe definition of the edge cover number utilizes `minUpTo`, which finds the smallest *k* for which an edge cover of size *k* exists. The definition requires an edge cover finset, where each vertex has an adjacent edge within that finset.\n\nObserving that the parameter *s* within edgeCoverNumber is a subset of the graph's edge set strengthens the approach. `minUpTo` guarantees the returned value, edgeCoverNumber, will be the size of some actual edge cover, or the maximum size of the graph, if no cover exists. The task now involves proving that for all edge covers and independent sets, the independent set's size is less than or equal to the cover size.\n\n\n**Formalizing Vertex-Edge Coverage**\n\nConsidering a relation between the independent set $I$ and edge cover $s$, where $(v, e) \\in R$ if and only if $v \\in e$. Since $s$ is an edge cover, every vertex $v$ in $I$ is covered by at least one edge $e$ in $s$.\n\nEvery edge *e* in *s* can contain at most one vertex of *I*. If an edge contained two vertices from *I*, it would violate the independence property. This means edges and vertices are associated in a 1-to-1 or 0-to-1 way.\n\nThis leads to the conclusion that each edge covers at most one element of *I*. Therefore, by constructing a mapping from the independent set to the edge cover, the cardinality of the independent set cannot exceed that of the edge cover.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/check.sh Challenge_1facfd37.lean","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_1facfd37","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Running check.sh on Challenge","toolSummary":"Run check.sh on initial challenge"}}]}
